    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Kīngitanga arose in the 1850s from Māori meetings that sought to unite iwi against the sale of their land, at a time when the number of European colonists was increasing rapidly.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potatau-te-wherowhero">Pōtatau Te Wherowhero</a> was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a> in 1858, and the movement took on the appearance of an alternative government, with its own flag, newspaper, bank, councillors, magistrates and law enforcement.</li><li>The colonial government invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> in 1863, 486,500 hectares were confiscated, and the movement survived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-country">King Country</a>; the Māori monarchy now operates outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> law.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In July 1881 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhiao">Tāwhiao</a> and 70 followers laid down their guns at Alexandra and placed beside them 70 roasted pigeons and a fantail, explaining, &quot;This means peace.&quot;</p></aside>
    </section>

